  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe latest radar and satellite imagery reveal a north-south \noriented band of lake effect rain showers between the I- 71 and \nI-77 corridors, aided by a Lake Huron moisture connection and \nsurface convergence. This activity proceeds the arrival of a \nchilly air mass, characterized by 850 mb temperatures falling \nnear -5 to -6 degrees C later this morning as high pressure \nbuilds east into the region. Lake effect rain showers should \ngradually diminish through this morning as more dry air and \nsubsidence enter the area.\n\nHave gone ahead and upgraded the Freeze Watch area to a Freeze \nWarning with clear skies and generally light winds expected \novernight into Sunday morning. Additionally, have issued a Frost \nAdvisory for Lake County and the Ashtabula and Erie, PA lakeshores. \nA bit of a tricky Frost/Freeze forecast, especially west of the I-71 \ncorridor and along the northeast lakeshore as an increasing pressure \ngradient (925 mb winds approaching 10 to 15 knots) and developing \nland breeze could result in slightly higher surface winds and warmer \ntemperatures. However, given forecast dew points in the upper 20s to \nlower 30 and a favorable environment for radiative cooling (at least \ninitially), went on the more aggressive side for Frost/Freeze \nheadlines.\n\nDepending on which areas hit Freeze criteria tonight, there is the \npotential for another round of Frost Sunday night into Monday as the \nhigh pressure center slowly shifts east into southeast Ohio, \nresulting in low temperatures in the low to mid-30s. A gradual \nwarming of temperatures (slightly above normal) and dry conditions \nare expected through next week as high pressure continues to \ninfluence the weather pattern.",
